records.qn_text:
(a) whether Government has assessed the impact of heatwaves, erratic monsoon, flash floods, cyclones and cloudbursts on agriculture, public health, infrastructure and the economy during the last three years;
(b) the State-wise details of casualties, economic losses and damage caused by such extreme weather events, along with forecast accuracy and effectiveness of early warning systems;
(c) whether Government proposes to modernize IMD through nationwide Doppler weather radar coverage, AI-driven forecasting and district-level weather warnings; and
(d) the details of the national strategy, timeline and financial outlay for Himalayan glacial lake monitoring, earthquake and tsunami preparedness, the Deep Ocean Mission and coastal resilience?

vg

(a) whether Government has assessed the justification for continued toll collection on National Highways after recovery of project costs and whether any independent audit of toll plazas, FASTag collections and concession agreements has been conducted during  last five years;
(b) the number of National Highway projects delayed, project-wise, reasons therefor and penalties imposed and recovered from defaulting contractors;
(c) whether Government has assessed the impact of rising toll charges and fuel costs on transport operators, farmers and inflation; and
(d) whether Government proposes to rationalise toll charges and introduce a transparent, time-bound toll policy with public disclosure of toll revenues and concession periods?

(a) whether Government has achieved the targets under the National Sickle Cell Elimination Mission (NSCEM), particularly in Maharashtra and other high-burden States, if so, the details of screening, diagnosis and treatment during the last three years State-wise;
(b) the details of support proposed for free screening camps through medical institutions and voluntary organisations and measures to strengthen coordination with district health authorities for timely detection and follow-up of patients; and
(c) the status of the nationwide digital platform for real-time registration and monitoring of sickle cell patients and if not operational, the reasons therefor and the timeline for its implementation?

(a) whether Government is aware of complaints of higher electricity bills after installation of smart prepaid meters compared to conventional meters;
(b) whether any independent technical audit has verified the accuracy and billing methodology of smart meters, if so, the details thereof;
(c) the State-wise number of consumer complaints on smart meter billing during the last three years and action taken thereon;
(d) whether Government proposes to provide consumers the option of independent meter testing and conventional meters in unresolved billing disputes; and
(e) the safeguards and grievance redressal mechanisms to prevent erroneous billing and protect consumer interests under the Smart Meter National Programme?

(a) whether Government has achieved the objectives of the PM SVANidhi Scheme; 
(b) if so, the details of loans sanctioned, disbursed, repayment rates and beneficiaries during the last five years, year-wise and State-wise, including Maharashtra;
(c) the number of street vendors who have availed the second and third loan tranches, State-wise, and the criteria adopted for assessing their creditworthiness;
(d) whether Government has assessed the Scheme's impact on vendors' income, digital payments and financial inclusion, if so, the details thereof; and
(e) the measures taken to expand PM SVANidhi coverage in small towns, peri-urban areas and major markets, particularly in the State of Maharashtra?

(a) whether India's increasing dependence on imported strategic industrial goods poses a serious economic and national security risk;
(b) the sectors where import dependence exceeds 50 per cent and the annual import bill incurred thereon;
(c) whether any time-bound roadmap has been prepared to achieve self-reliance in these sectors, indicating annual targets and budgetary support; and
(d) the measurable outcomes achieved under 'Make in India' and PLI Schemes in terms of domestic value addition, employment generation and export growth?

(a) the total expenditure incurred on the development of India's first hydrogen-powered train, including rolling stock and hydrogen infrastructure;
(b) whether any comparative techno-economic study has been conducted between hydrogen, battery-electric and fully electrified trains in terms of cost, efficiency, emissions and long term viability, if so, the details thereof;
(c) the details of hydrogen train projects approved and proposed across the country, route-wise;
(d) the availability and cost of green hydrogen for such projects; and
(e) the timeline for expansion, indigenous manufacturing achieved, expected operational and environmental benefits of the project?

(a) whether Government has notified new rules under the Telecommunications Act, 2023 and reviewed their impact on consumers, telecom operators and citizens;
(b) the details of concerns received regarding possible misuse of surveillance provisions, protection of personal data and citizens’ right to privacy;
(c) the outcome of any independent assessment conducted on cybersecurity preparedness of telecom networks, prevention of digital frauds and protection of critical communication infrastructure;
(d) the impact of these rules on small telecom providers, competition and consumer interests in the telecom sector; and
(e) the measures taken to ensure transparency, accountability and protection of citizens’ rights while implementing the new telecom framework?

(a) whether Government has reviewed the existing spectrum auction model and evaluated its impact on telecom affordability, competition and public revenue;
(b) whether Government has examined a revenue-sharing mechanism wherein a fixed share of telecom operators’ consumer billing revenue is contributed to the exchequer instead of relying mainly on spectrum auctions;
(c) whether any assessment has been made regarding the impact of such a model on consumer tariffs, telecom investment and service quality; and
(d) the steps taken to ensure that spectrum, a public resource, benefits citizens through affordable connectivity and transparent revenue generation?

(a) the steps taken to address the acute distress faced by dairy farmers, particularly in Maharashtra, who are reportedly selling milk below production cost due to rising feed, labour and input expenses;
(b) whether Government has assessed the impact of falling procurement prices, export uncertainty and seasonal oversupply on farmers' livelihoods;
(c) the details of synthetic milk and adulteration cases detected during the last three years, State-wise, and action taken against the offenders;
(d) the impact of restrictions on loose milk sales on small vendors and consumers; and
(e) the measures proposed to ensure remunerative milk prices, curb adulteration and protect dairy farmers' livelihoods?

(a) whether Government is aware of misleading advertisements by certain EdTech platforms promising "100 percent placements", high-paying jobs and unauthorised institutional affiliations;
(b) whether complaints have been received regarding fake Universities, unaccredited foreign tie-ups, invalid degrees and deceptive refund or loan policies during the last three years, if so, the details thereof;
(c) the action taken against such entities under the relevant laws;
(d) whether Government proposes stricter regulation of EdTech advertisements, placement claims and online educational institutions; and
(e) the measures taken to protect students from fraudulent educational platforms and degree mills?

(a) whether Government has assessed if Maharashtra's allocation under the Scheme for Special Assistance to States for Capital Investment (SASCI) is proportionate to its contribution to India's GDP, manufacturing output and tax revenues;
(b) the details of funds allocated, sanctioned, released and utilised by Maharashtra since 2020-21, year-wise;
(c) whether any independent audit has evaluated if SASCI has created additional capital assets rather than substituting States' own capital expenditure; and
(d) whether Government proposes to revise the allocation formula and enhance Maharashtra's share to address its growing infrastructure deficit and regional imbalances?

(a) whether Government has taken cognisance of the petition before the Bombay High Court seeking a CBI probe into the alleged extraction of natural gas from ONGC's Krishna-Godavari Basin, reportedly causing a loss of about US$1.55 billion;
(b) the findings of the A.P. Shah Committee, the DeGolyer & MacNaughton (D&M) audit and other investigations;
(c) whether the claim of migratory gas legally permits extraction from adjoining licensed blocks;
(d) the steps taken to protect public sector hydrocarbon assets and prevent unauthorised extraction; and
(e) whether Government proposes to recover losses and initiate legal action against those found responsible, if any?

(a) whether the outstanding debt of the Delhi Metro Rail Corporation (DMRC) has exceeded ?30,000 crore and if so, the lender-wise details, including liabilities to Japan International Cooperation Agency (JICA), as on date;
(b) whether Government has assessed DMRC's capacity to service its existing and Phase-IV debt from operational revenues;
(c) details of financial assistance, including equity, grants and subordinate loans, provided by the Centre and Government of NCT during the last five years; and
(d) whether the mounting debt burden has resulted in or is likely to result in, higher metro fares and the measures proposed to ensure DMRC's financial sustainability without burdening commuters?

(a) whether Government is aware that heavy rainfall and waterlogging in Mumbai repeatedly disrupt suburban and long-distance railway services, causing cancellation, delays and severe inconvenience to lakhs of commuters;
(b) the number of trains cancelled, diverted and delayed during the recent monsoon, along with the financial loss incurred;
(c) whether recurring flooding exposes serious deficiencies in railway drainage, track resilience and disaster preparedness despite substantial investments indicating the details of funds sanctioned and utilised during the last five years on various projects; and
(d) the corrective measures, timeline and funds earmarked to modernise vulnerable railway infrastructure and ensure uninterrupted services during future monsoons?

(a) whether Government has formulated a comprehensive diplomatic strategy to safeguard India's strategic, energy and economic interests amid the ongoing Iran–USA–Gulf conflict;
(b) whether India has undertaken any diplomatic initiatives to promote de-escalation while maintaining balanced relations with Iran, the Gulf countries, Israel and the United States;
(c) the details of consultations held with major regional and global partners on ensuring uninterrupted energy supplies and maritime security; and
(d) whether Government proposes to prepare a long-term National West Asia Strategy to address emerging geopolitical challenges and protect India's strategic autonomy?

(a) whether Government has taken cognisance of the recurring collapse, caving-in and structural failures of newly constructed bridges, flyovers and National Highway projects across the country, including the recent incidents on the Delhi–Mumbai Expressway and other major infrastructure projects;
(b) the findings of investigations into these failures, including responsibility fixed on contractors, consultants and officials and the details of penalties, blacklisting, recoveries and criminal action initiated; and
(c) whether Government proposes to mandate independent third-party structural audits before inauguration and establish a national accountability framework with strict quality standards to prevent recurrence of such failures?

(a) the post-wise and department-wise number of contractual employees engaged by the New Delhi Municipal Council (NDMC) and the total period of their contractual engagement;
(b) the department-wise remuneration of contractual employees and the corresponding pay scales of regular employees performing similar duties;
(c) the steps taken to ensure the principle of "equal pay for equal work" and to regularise eligible contractual employees; and
(d) whether there is any difference in the pay structure and service conditions of contractual doctors and other contractual employees in NDMC, and if so, the reasons therefor?

ak

(a) whether Government has assessed the impact of global supply disruptions on fertilizer availability, prices and cultivation costs during 2025–26, and the details of shortages, State-wise;
(b) if so, the complaints received regarding black marketing, hoarding and coercive tagging of subsidised fertilizers with non-subsidised products, and the action taken against erring dealers;
(c) whether Government has reviewed the use of hazardous pesticides and seed-treatment chemicals, their impact on farmers' health, exports and the environment and the steps taken to strengthen quality and residue standards; and
(d) the measures taken to ensure timely fertilizer availability, prevent profiteering and affordable agricultural inputs for farmers?

J

(a) whether Government has assessed the potential of Dimethyl Ether (DME) as an indigenous blending fuel with LPG to reduce import dependence and strengthen India's energy security;
(b) whether Government proposes to formulate a national DME blending policy and provide fiscal incentives, viability gap funding and regulatory support for DME production from coal, biomass, agricultural residues, municipal waste and other indigenous feedstock; and
(c) the details of pilot projects, procurement initiatives by Public Sector Oil Marketing Companies (OMC) and the estimated impact of large-scale DME adoption on LPG imports, employment generation and indigenous manufacturing under the Make in India initiative?

(a) whether Government has examined the smart on-site waste processing model recently introduced in Bengaluru and assessed its effectiveness in scientific waste management, reducing landfill burden and improving urban sanitation;
(b) whether Government proposes to replicate similar decentralised waste-processing systems in other metropolitan cities; and
(c) the details of financial, technical and institutional assistance proposed to be provided to Urban Local Bodies for adoption of such systems under various urban development and waste management schemes?

(a) whether Government is aware that Artificial Intelligence (AI) data centres are depleting groundwater in water-stressed cities like Bengaluru while farmers and citizens struggle for water;
(b) the estimated water consumption by data centres in 2025-26 and projected demand by 2030;
(c) whether any national policy exists to restrict such centres in drought-prone areas and mandate recycled water use;
(d) the details of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA) done for these projects; and
(e) the steps taken to prevent corporate water grab and ensure priority for common citizens over big tech companies?

(a) whether Government has assessed the impact of toxic industrial effluents on rivers, groundwater, drinking water and agricultural land, including in Maharashtra and if so, the details thereof;
(b) whether heavy metals such as arsenic, lead, chromium, cadmium and nickel have been detected above permissible limits in major river basins during the last five years, and whether vulnerable villages have been identified;
(c) the number of industries found violating effluent discharge norms and the action taken against them; and
(d) whether Government proposes to strengthen real-time water quality monitoring and pollution control measures to safeguard public health, agriculture and water resources?

(a) whether Government has assessed the strategic implications of China's expanding ownership, financing and operational control of ports in neighbouring countries and the Indian Ocean Region on India's national security, maritime interests and geopolitical influence;
(b) whether Government has identified risks to India's sea lines of communication, trade, energy security and strategic interests arising therefrom;
(c) whether any inter-ministerial review or White Paper has been prepared on China's maritime footprint; and
(d) the diplomatic, strategic and economic measures taken during the last five years to safeguard India's interests and leadership in the Indian Ocean Region?

(a) whether Government is aware that India continues to record one of the world's highest snakebite fatalities despite the availability of Anti-Snake Venom (ASV);
(b) the number of snakebite cases, deaths and ASV shortages in PHCs, CHCs and District Hospitals during the last three years, State-wise;
(c) whether Government has assessed the adequacy of domestic ASV production, including the capacity of public sector manufacturers such as Haffkine Bio-Pharmaceutical Corporation Ltd, and the need for financial support; and
(d) the measures being taken to strengthen ASV production, develop region-specific antivenoms, improve emergency treatment and achieve the target of reducing snakebite deaths by 2030?

(a) whether Government has assessed the need to mandate repair-friendly smartphones, including replaceable batteries, availability of spare parts, repair manuals and software support, in line with global best practices, to protect consumer interests and reduce electronic waste;
(b) whether Government proposes to enact a comprehensive Right to Repair framework for smartphones and other electronic devices, prescribe repairability and battery durability standards and require manufacturers to disclose battery lifespan and repair costs; and
(c) the details of consultations held with stakeholders and the steps taken or proposed to promote affordable repairs, recycling, refurbishment and sustainable electronic manufacturing in the country?

(a) whether Government has assessed the potential of agricultural residues for electricity generation and production of renewable fuels through advanced biomass technologies;
(b) details of incentives, financial assistance and policy support provided for biomass-based power projects, waste-to-energy plants and biomass collection, storage and transportation infrastructure;
(c) whether thermal power plants have been directed to enhance co-firing of biomass pellets to reduce stubble burning and air pollution and if so, the details thereof; and
(d) targets fixed for biomass-based power generation, reduction in carbon emissions, employment generation and utilisation of agricultural residues under various schemes during the last three years, year-wise?

(a) the total number of National Highway projects currently under implementation across the country;
(b) the number of such projects that are pending or delayed along with the estimated cost involved, particularly in the State of Maharashtra;
(c) the major reasons for delay or pendency of these projects, and;
(d) whether Government proposes to promote sustainable and environmentally friendly transport alternatives such as metro rail, monorail, rapid train systems or sky bus corridors along major highways to reduce environmental impact; and
(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, reasons therefor?

(a) whether any assessment has been made regarding the impact of global conflicts on India’s coal supply chain, imports and energy security;
(b) the steps taken to ensure uninterrupted coal supply to thermal power plants in view of possible disruptions in imports from countries such as Australia and Indonesia;
(c) whether companies such as Coal India Limited have been directed to increase domestic coal production to meet shortages;
(d) the measures taken to address rising coal prices and transportation challenges affecting industries; and
(e) whether any long-term strategy has been formulated to strengthen India’s coal reserves and safeguard the power sector during global energy disruptions?

(a) whether any assessment has been made regarding the impact of continuing conflicts in Asia and the Gulf on the safety of Indian civil aviation and international routes passing through hubs such as Dubai International airport;
(b) whether Indian airlines operating from airports such as Indira Gandhi International airport have been advised to reroute flights to avoid potential missile or drone threats;
(c) details of safety advisories issued by Directorate General of Civil Aviation to ensure passenger and crew safety;
(d) the financial impact on airlines such as Air India and IndiGo; and
(e) steps taken to ensure uninterrupted international connectivity and safety of passenger?

(a) project-wise details of proposals received from Government of Maharashtra under industrial corridors and logistics parks/multimodal logistics parks, upto the year 2025, indicating the concerned corridor/scheme, specific project/node, viz. Shendra-Bidkin Industrial Area, Dighi Port Industrial Area, proposed logistics hubs in Nagpur-Wardha, Navi Mumbai-Pune etc. and year of submission/proposal;
(b) status of Central approvals, including amounts approved and investments mobilized/committed, scheme-wise and year-wise, for these proposals from Maharashtra under National Industrial Corridor Development Programme (NICDP) or related schemes; and
(c) region-wise distribution of approved projects and investments in Maharashtra, with special emphasis on backward regions including any pending proposals or imbalances in allocation?

(a) whether Government will place in the public domain, all concession agreements executed with private toll operators, including project cost, concession period, traffic projections, rate escalation formula, revenue-sharing terms and penalty clauses;
(b) the project-wise details of cumulative toll collected vis-à-vis sanctioned project cost and approved return and whether any toll plazas continue to collect toll beyond recovery of cost and agreed returns;
(c) the number of contract renegotiations undertaken during the last five years and their financial implications; and
(d) whether independent audits of toll collections have been conducted and if so, whether such audit reports will be made public?

(a) whether Government has assessed concerns raised regarding the safety, monitoring and informed consent processes in the ongoing HPV vaccination programme;
(b) the details of adverse events reported, if any and the findings of independent investigations conducted thereon;
(c) whether the indigenous HPV vaccine introduced under the national immunisation framework has undergone full clinical trials and regulatory approval by the competent authority;
(d) the mechanism in place for post-vaccination surveillance and transparency in public reporting; and
(e) the steps taken to address public apprehensions and ensure informed participation while preventing misinformation?

(a) whether Government has taken note of reports that airfares between India and major West Asian destinations such as Dubai, Doha, Abu Dhabi and Muscat have increased three to four times in recent weeks;
(b) whether Government has assessed the reasons for this sharp increase, including war-risk insurance premiums, aviation turbine fuel price volatility and flight rerouting due to regional conflict;
(c) whether any regulatory mechanism exists to prevent excessive fare escalation during such crises; and
(d) the steps being taken to protect Indian passengers, particularly migrant workers, from abnormal fare hikes?

(a) whether Government is aware that cotton farmers in Maharashtra are facing distress during the 2025–26 season due to market prices falling below MSP and delays and limitations in procurement by the Cotton Corporation of India (CCI);
(b) details of MSP fixed and total quantity procured by CCI in Maharashtra during the last three years, year-wise and district-wise and the quantity still remaining unsold with farmers;
(c) whether Government has received representations regarding losses due to imports, procurement delays and procedural barriers; and
(d) the steps taken to ensure full procurement at MSP and protect Maharashtra’s cotton farmers from distress sale and financial hardship?

bk

(a) whether Government has assessed the geopolitical and national security implications of foreign-owned undersea data cables connecting India with other countries, particularly regarding data sovereignty, surveillance risks and strategic dependence;
(b) whether existing encryption standards, regulatory safeguards and oversight mechanisms adequately protect India’s critical data flows and Artificial Intelligence (AI) infrastructure from external interference or control;
(c) the steps taken to strengthen indigenous capabilities in AI, high-performance computing and semiconductors to reduce technological dependence;
(d) India’s preparedness in defence AI in comparision with major powers; and
(e) the diplomatic and multilateral initiatives undertaken to safeguard India’s strategic autonomy and leadership in global AI governance?

(a) whether despite having the world’s largest youth population, India continues to lag behind major sporting nations in Olympic performance, grassroots participation and sports science support;
(b) whether a time-bound national roadmap exists to make structured sports education compulsory in schools and systematically identify talent at the district level;
(c) the concrete steps taken to reform coaching standards and curb administrative inefficiencies and politicisation within sports federations;
(d) the safeguards in place to ensure financial security, insurance and post-career rehabilitation for athletes; and
(e) the specific targets set to transform India into a global sporting power within the next decade?

(a) whether Government is aware of alarming rise in violent attacks and racial assaults on Indian nationals abroad particualrly in Canada, USA, UK, Bangladesh, which strike at India’s culture, religion and national dignity;
(b) if so, the details thereof during the period from 2023 to 2025, including hate-motivated cases country-wise;
(c) the urgent diplomatic, consular and bilateral measures taken to ensure safety, faster investigations and protection of Indian citizens overseas; and
(d) the steps proposed to strengthen safety advisories, emergency support and global advocacy against xenophobia targeting Indians?

(a) the details of steps taken to introduce industry and start-up-oriented courses in IITs, IIMs and other higher educational institutions to promote entrepreneurship and job creation;
(b) the manner in which practical training on industry setup, investment, marketing, exports and Government schemes such as Startup India and MSME is being incorporated;
(c) the existing provisions to facilitate participation of students and start-ups from these institutions in Central Government tenders, including infrastructure, IT and services; and
(d) the measures taken to support student entrepreneurs in establishing startups and generating employment in the country?

(a) whether Government is aware that despite strong demand in rural, tribal and backward districts of Maharashtra such as Gadchiroli and Nandurbar, no new Kendriya Vidyalayas have been sanctioned due to non-receipt of so-called “feasible proposals” and the reasons for such inaction;
(b) the status of the existing Kendriya Vidyalayas in Maharashtra, including enrolment capacity, infrastructure gaps, teacher vacancies and unmet demand for affordable CBSE education, district-wise; and
(c) the concrete steps being taken to remove land and funding barriers, ensure State-Centre coordination and expand Kendriya Vidyalayas in aspirational and neglected regions of Maharashtra?

(a) the concrete steps taken by Government/FSSAI to curb widespread adulteration in essential food items such as milk, ghee, spices, honey, paneer and others, which pose a serious public health crisis;
(b) whether Government has taken note of persistent enforcement weaknesses despite repeated concerns raised;
(c) the manner in which these enforcement gaps are linked to erosion of trust among consumers in domestic food products and damage to the credibility of genuine farmers producing safe staples; and
(d) the specific measures proposed to strengthen enforcement, such as increased manpower, harsher penalties, rapid testing infrastructure, better traceability to restore agricultural integrity and public confidence?

(a) whether Government recognises that rising climate concerns and rapid urbanisation demand urgent measures to shift people from private vehicles to reliable and affordable public transport;
(b) whether it is a fact that lack of fare and information integration across metro, bus and other transport systems is discouraging commuters from using public transport;
(c) the reasons for continued dependence on informal and unregulated transport modes due to gaps in formal public transport systems; and
(d) the concrete and time-bound steps taken to implement fare integration, strengthen public transport infrastructure and ensure affordable, accessible and sustainable mobility for citizens?

(a) whether persistent concerns exist among rice farmers and mill owners in various parts of the country due to export restrictions on non-basmati in the past causing surplus, warehouse congestion, storage shortages, depressed demand and income losses;
(b) status of rice export policy as of 2026, including APEDA registration norms for non-basmati, its effects on domestic prices, farmer incomes and mill operations in surplus regions;
(c) whether Punjab’s Basmati sector continues to face distress from payment delays, ?2,000+ crore stranded consignments and Iran market uncertainties; and
(d) steps proposed—including diplomacy, storage aid, incentives and market diversification—to resolve these issues and protect affected livelihoods?

(a) whether Government is aware that allocation for agricultural research and education has declined in real terms despite rising climate risks, input costs and low farm incomes;
(b) whether the allocation of ?1.32 lakh crore to the Ministry, when agriculture supports nearly 46 percent of the workforce, reflects adequate priority, given that most funds are absorbed by subsidies rather than productivity investments; and
(c) the reasons as to why expenditure on agricultural education and research has been kept at about ?9,967 crore despite claims of promoting technology led farming and whether continued underinvestment threatens long-term food security and farmer livelihoods?

(a) whether the Labour Codes, allow layoffs of up to 300 workers without prior approval, restrict strike rights with mandatory 14-day notice and mandate 51 per cent union membership for sole bargaining, thereby diluting job security, collective bargaining and post-independence worker protections;
(b) if so, the steps taken to address nationwide protests including the February 12, 2026 Bharat Bandh demanding rollback and concerns over gig/contract workers' inadequate social security; and
(c) the measures to resolve ambiguities in wage definitions, State-level enforcement variations, SME compliance burdens and stagnant Ministry's budget allocation amid rising unemployment and inequality?

(a) whether Government has reviewed compliance of Colleges and Universities in Maharashtra with norms prescribed by the University Grants Commission, particularly regarding faculty strength, infrastructure and teaching quality;
(b) the number of sanctioned teaching posts lying vacant, extent of reliance on ad-hoc or contractual teachers and deficiencies in laboratory and digital infrastructure during the last five years;
(c) whether central grants released for higher education in Maharashtra have lapsed or have remained unutilised; and
(d) the steps taken or proposed, with timelines, to fill vacancies, upgrade infrastructure, enforce UGC regulations and improve teaching quality and employability of graduate and engineering students in the State?

(a) whether Gold holdings of the Reserve Bank of India increased from about 557.75 metric tonnes as on 31 March 2014 to about 879.58 metric tonnes as on 31 March 2025, a net addition of about 321.8 metric tonnes;
(b) whether currency in circulation during the same period rose from about ?13 lakh crore to ?36.8 lakh crore;
(c) whether Government/RBI acknowledge that currency in circulation nearly tripled while Gold reserves grew at a much slower pace; and
(d) the economic rationale for this divergence and any assessment of its implications for monetary stability and confidence in the Indian rupee?

(a) whether Government acknowledges that despite higher housing sales value, the number of units sold has declined, indicating a shift towards luxury housing and exclusion of middle- and lower-income buyers;
(b) the reasons for the share of affordable housing falling from about 38 per cent in 2019 to below 20 per cent in recent years, despite 'Housing for All' commitments;
(c) the current estimate of urban housing shortage and measures proposed to address projections of nearly 3 crore units by 2030; and
(d) whether a city-wise utilisation and outcome audit of PMAY-Urban has been conducted to assess delivery, occupancy, delays and cost overruns?

(a) when Government will stop shielding airlines from sky-high ATF taxes, push sustainable fuels and force real hedging so passengers aren’t crushed by every global oil spike;
(b) measures Government is taking to fix broken airports, upgrade air traffic control, audit unsafe airstrips and end the daily torture of cracked seats and extortionate fares on long-haul flights; and
(c) with the duopoly jacking up fares during every crisis and festival, will Government finally impose fare bands, a clear index and hard curbs on predatory dynamic pricing to protect every Indian flyer?

(a) the comparative details of distance, estimated cost, land acquisition, travel time, and economic impact of the originally proposed Nashik–Pune railway alignment via Sangamner and the revised alignment approved by Indian Railways;
(b) whether land acquisition or compensation processes had already commenced along the Sangamner alignment, if so, the present status thereof;
(c) whether any socio-economic impact assessment was carried out before altering the alignment, particularly for farmers, students, workers, and small businesses in the affected talukas; and
(d) the steps taken so far by Government to address public opposition and ensure transparency in decision-making for the project?

(a) whether Government has reviewed concerns that India’s foreign policy is projecting conflicting signals under the rubric of strategic autonomy;
(b) how continued energy trade and military engagements with Russia are being reconciled with India’s commitment to a rules-based international order and partnerships with western nations;
(c) whether Government has assessed if such positioning has weakened trust among partners and reduced India’s influence in its immediate neighbourhood; and
(d) the concrete diplomatic, economic and strategic measures proposed to counter growing external influence in South Asia and ensure a proactive and coherent foreign policy roadmap?

(a) whether Government is aware that the combined tax revenue of the Union and the States in Financial Year 2024-25, when averaged across the population, amounts to approximately ?51,000 per citizen annually, along with the trend in per-capita tax collections over the last five years;
(b) the extent to which this burden is increasingly realised through indirect taxes such as GST, Petroleum Levies, Cesses and Surcharges affecting citizens irrespective of income and the assessment made of the equity implications thereof; and
(c) the measures proposed to broaden the direct-tax base and ensure transparency in the utilisation of per-citizen tax revenues?

(a) whether the Central Government has received proposals from the State Government of Maharashtra seeking central financial assistance for river-linking and major irrigation projects to make the State drought-free, including Daman Ganga–Ekdare–Godavari, Daman Ganga–Vaitarna–Godavari, Konkan–Godavari Basin, Nal Ganga–Wainganga and Par–Godavari projects and the present status of their approval and fund release;
(b) whether 29 irrigation projects have been included under the Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchayee Yojana and their basin-wise progress; and
(c) whether ten out of thirteen Krishna and Godavari basin projects have been completed and the timeline for completion of the remaining projects, including the ?2,038 crore Nilwande-II proposal covering 64,260 hectares?

(a) the details of pending crop insurance claims of farmers in Maharashtra, particularly in Parbhani district, where claims amounting to about ?200–225 crore payable to nearly two lakh soybean farmers remain unsettled despite directions issued by the National Technical Advisory Committee;
(b) the reasons for delay in disbursement of the said claims even after rejection of objections raised by the concerned insurance company regarding crop-cutting experiments; and
(c) the steps taken or proposed to ensure time-bound settlement of crop insurance claims and to fix accountability on insurance companies and officials responsible for such delays during 2025–26?

(a) number of districts and farmers in Maharashtra identified under the Pradhan Mantri Dhan Dhaanya Krishi Yojana (PM-DDKY) out of the 100 low-productivity districts, and specific support proposed for drought-prone regions such as Marathwada and Vidarbha;
(b) role of State Agricultural Universities and public research institutions in Maharashtra under National Mission on High Yielding Seeds, including the number of climate-resilient seed varieties developed or proposed for the State; and
(c) the production and procurement targets for Tur, Urad and Masoor in Maharashtra under the Aatmanirbharta in Pulses Mission and the manner in which the ?1,000 crore allocation will support farmer incomes during 2025–26?

(a) whether the Ministry has assessed the reasons for India’s relatively low foreign tourist arrivals compared to destinations such as Singapore and leading European countries and the key findings thereof;
(b) whether issues relating to tourist safety, flight disruptions, uneven infrastructure and fragmented destination promotion have adversely affected India’s tourism image abroad;
(c) how India’s visa facilitation and entry processes compare with global best practices; and
(d) whether the Ministry proposes reforms such as regulatory simplification, single-window clearances, coordinated global promotion, aviation coordination and enhanced safety measures to increase foreign tourist arrivals and tourism-related foreign exchange earnings and if so, the timeline thereof?

(a) whether Government is aware that most telecom recharge plans compulsorily bundle mobile data with voice calls, leading to higher costs for consumers who do not use the internet;
(b) whether this practice disproportionately affects poor households, senior citizens and basic phone users who require only calling facilities;
(c) whether the Telecom Regulatory Authority of India has examined the affordability impact of mandatory data bundling on vulnerable sections;
(d) whether Government proposes to mandate or encourage telecom service providers to offer affordable, voice-only recharge packs nationwide; and
